alex: quick heads-up — hitting a nasty edge in the C# SDK when using funcall + streaming. If a function output schema has an optional field, the streaming JSON sometimes yields "null" for that key instead of omitting it, and System.Text.Json with nullable reference types turned on throws during deserialization.

alex: minimal repro: call a tool that returns { "summary": null } vs {} in the streaming chunks. Deserialize into a record with string? Summary { get; init; } and it fails/flags a warning because we treat null as actual null not missing.

alex: stack trace/snippet:```
System.Text.Json.JsonException: The JSON value could not be converted to System.String. Path: $.result.summary | at System.Text.Json...
// stream chunk example (simplified)
{"event":"output_delta","delta":{"result":{"summary":null}}}
{"event":"output_delta","delta":{"result":{"detail":"ok"}}}
```

maria: thanks — can you paste the exact deserialization call you use? are you using our streaming helpers or raw HttpClient?

alex: using Redwood.CSharp.StreamingClient.ReadResponsesAsync and then JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Result>(payload) in the message handler. I can share a tiny repro later.

kevin: hmm, streaming can split object boundaries; sometimes we emit a partial object with keys then later fill them. Runtime should avoid writing explicit nulls for missing optional keys but iirc some tool wrappers write null placeholders for schema fields.

kevin: do we have any guarantees about whether funcall outputs must be canonicalized (missing vs null)? we might need to normalize at SDK layer.

maria: agreed — SDK should be permissive. We had similar issues in TS/Ruby where helpers coerced null->missing, but C# stricter. Propose an extension method that normalizes JsonElement before deserializing, turning explicit nulls for absent/optional keys into JsonDocument.ObjectWithoutThatProperty.

maria: quick implementation idea:```csharp
public static string PermissiveNormalize(string json)
{
  using var doc = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
  // walk tree, remove properties whose value is JsonValueKind.Null when the schema marks them optional
  // fallback: convert explicit nulls to missing for top-level result object
  return doc.RootElement.GetRawText();
}
```

sophia: care: we shouldn't remove intentionally null values when the model legitimately returns null (e.g., summary = null is a semantic null). But for typed outputs where schema says field is optional, null vs missing should be equivalent. Need schema-aware logic.

alex: yeah, my example is optional summary. Downstream apps treat missing==null fine. This mainly breaks when using non-nullable reference types in records.

maria: another approach: Deserialize into a permissive intermediary class (Dictionary<string, JsonElement>) and then map to typed model with TryGetProperty checks: treat missing or JsonValueKind.Null the same. That avoids destructive string surgery.

maria: psuedo helper:```csharp
public static T PermissiveDeserialize<T>(string json)
{
  var doc = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
  var root = doc.RootElement;
  // for each property expected on T, use root.TryGetProperty(propName, out prop)
  // if prop is JsonValueKind.Null or missing -> set default/null in target
  // else deserialize normally for that property
}
```

kevin: +1 to intermediary mapping. We can add this as a small helper in Redwood.CSharp.Sdk.Extensions and wire it into the streaming client path. Keep it opt-in for now behind a flag in StreamingOptions.PermissiveFuncallParsing.

alex: sounds good. Anyone wants to own a quick prototype? If not i can take a spike tomorrow.

maria: i can pick this up — will do prototype + unit tests (null vs missing vs explicit string) and a note about when to use permissive mode. i'll also add a CI test that runs against the funcall mock stream that emits null then later other keys.

ci-bot: test failure detected: tests/streaming/funcall_null_handling_test.cs failed on main (flaky?). See https://ci.internal/redwood/12345

maria: thanks CI — i'll reproduce locally and make the unit test deterministic.

sophia: runtime owners fyi: if runtime can avoid emitting explicit null tokens for optional absent keys that would be preferable; but we know some tool adapters produce nulls intentionally. won't block SDK fix, but it'd be great to document the canonical behavior.

kevin: logging note: let's add a debug log in the streaming client when we see JsonValueKind.Null for a known optional property so teams can triage if it's coming from runtime or tool.

alex: thanks all. maria — if you open the PR, tag me for code review and i'll run integration tests with an internal sample app.

maria: will do. action items: (1) PR with PermissiveDeserialize helper + opt-in flag, (2) unit tests covering null vs missing, (3) CI test update, (4) small runtime doc note. Assigning to me, ETA EOD tomorrow. :thumbsup:

alex: great — appreciate it :v:
